School Project – SharePoint-Based Helpdesk and Customer Chat System

1. Project Overview - 
I developed this project as part of my IT studies, with the aim of improving my school's existing helpdesk system using Microsoft SharePoint.
The idea was to create a more organized way for the school to manage support tickets and communicate with students and other users who need technical assistance. 
I wanted to explore how SharePoint could be extended with a chat feature that allows support staff to communicate directly with users through individual support tickets.
This would make it easier to request additional information, keep track of conversations, and ensure that all communication remains connected to the correct support case.
The solution should also be accessible on both desktop computers and mobile devices.

2. Project Goals - 
The main goal was to extend the existing SharePoint helpdesk with a chat feature that is simple to use, secure, and accessible to both support staff and users.
My objectives were to:
Create an individual chat conversation for each support ticket.
Connect every conversation to the correct ticket ID.
Store and display the conversation history.
Allow support staff to request additional information from users.
Allow users to reply directly through the chat.
Make the interface accessible on desktop computers and mobile devices.
Ensure that users can only access conversations they are authorized to view.
Protect personal information and internal support data.
Reduce the need to handle every follow-up through separate email conversations.

3. SharePoint Integration - 
I used the school's existing SharePoint helpdesk list as the starting point for the project.
The list contains the following columns:
Column
Purpose
ID
Identifies each support ticket.
Beskrivning
Contains the description of the support ticket.
Chatthistorik
Intended to store the conversation history for the ticket.
Created
Records when the ticket was created.
E-mailadress
Stores the user's email address.
My plan was to integrate the chat functionality into the existing ticket structure rather than create a completely separate helpdesk system.
Each conversation would be associated with a unique ticket ID. This would ensure that conversations remain separate,
even when the same user has submitted multiple support tickets.

4. Support Staff Functionality - 
I wanted to make it possible for support staff to manage customer conversations directly through the helpdesk.
The support interface should allow staff to:
View and open support tickets they are authorized to access.
Read ticket descriptions and relevant user information.
View the conversation history.
Send messages to users.
Request additional information when needed.
Read replies and keep track of communication related to each ticket.
The aim was to make communication easier and improve the process of investigating and resolving technical support issues.

5. Customer Chat Interface - 
Another important part of the project was planning a separate and straightforward interface for users.
Users should be able to open their support conversation, read previous messages, and send replies without needing access to the internal helpdesk interface.
The customer interface should display the relevant conversation, allow messages to be sent and received, and show previous messages in chronological order.
I also wanted the interface to work in a standard web browser on both desktop computers and mobile devices.
Users must not be able to access other users' support tickets, email addresses, conversations, internal notes, or support management functions.

6. Microsoft Teams and External Users - 
One of my requirements was that users should not need Microsoft Teams to communicate with support staff.
I wanted to explore the possibility of providing access through a separate web interface or customer portal. 
This would make the solution more accessible to users who do not have a Microsoft Teams account.
Microsoft Teams could still be used internally for notifications or communication between support staff. 
Email notifications could also be added to inform users when support staff have replied.

7. Proposed Technologies - 
I explored how different Microsoft 365 technologies could be combined to build the solution.
SharePoint: To store and manage support tickets and integrate with the existing helpdesk.
Power Apps: As a possible way to build the internal support interface.
Power Automate: To automate notifications and other helpdesk processes.
Microsoft Teams: For optional internal notifications and communication.
A customer portal or web application: To provide external users with secure access to their own conversations.
The final technology choice would depend on security requirements, licensing, authentication, and integration possibilities.

8. Security and Access Control - 
Security was one of the most important considerations in the project.
I wanted to ensure that users could only access conversations they were authorized to view. This requires proper authentication, authorization, 
and data-source permissions rather than relying only on filtering tickets by email address in the user interface.
Internal support notes should also be separated from customer-visible messages to prevent sensitive information from being exposed.
The solution would need to be tested to ensure that users could not access other tickets by changing ticket IDs, URLs, or request parameters.

9. Responsive Design - 
I wanted the interface to work well across different screen sizes.
On desktop computers, the chat should provide enough space to read the conversation and write messages comfortably. 
On mobile devices, the layout should adapt to smaller screens while keeping the conversation readable and the message input easy to access.
The aim was to create a consistent and user-friendly experience regardless of which device the user chooses.

10. Potential Future Improvements
After implementing the basic functionality, the system could be expanded with additional features, such as:
Automatic email notifications when new messages arrive.
Message timestamps.
Unread message indicators.
File and image attachments.
Ticket status updates.
Improved search and filtering for support staff.
A clear separation between internal notes and customer messages.

11. Project Outcome and Learning - 
The goal of this school project was to explore how the existing SharePoint helpdesk could be improved by integrating a chat system for support tickets.
Through the project, I gained practical experience in planning IT solutions, working with Microsoft 365 technologies, considering system integration, 
and identifying security and access-control requirements.
The project also helped me understand the challenges involved in designing a customer-facing interface that is both user-friendly and secure.
The intended outcome was a more integrated helpdesk solution where support tickets and their conversations could be managed in one place, making it easier for the school to organize support requests and communicate with users.
